1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:02,920
In Jesus' name we pray. Amen.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,200
You guys are going to need to push back your lunch reservations after that time of worship because I'm ready to go.

3
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:41,820
No, I'm just kidding.

4
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:43,280
Kidding, kidding.

5
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,540
Hey, for those of you that haven't met me yet, my name is Ryan.

6
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:47,340
I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here.

7
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:48,320
And we are continuing.

8
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:49,060
I know.

9
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:49,860
I know.

10
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:51,840
We're in the homestretch.

11
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:57,680
But we are continuing our very long summer series on the life, ministry, and teachings of the Apostle Paul.

12
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:04,640
This is week 12, and just to break it up, just to give you some hope, some light at the end of the tunnel,

13
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:10,520
we are kicking off this morning kind of a three-week mini-series as we kind of wrap up this series.

14
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:15,920
We're going to be talking about Paul's view of salvation, which I'm really excited about.

15
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,420
I hope this series has been helpful.

16
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:22,080
I hope it's helped you see Paul's writing in the greater context of Paul's ministry.

17
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:30,980
His letters were not written as a direct message from God to you and me for us to cut and paste very small sections of them

18
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:35,100
and put them on coffee mugs and refrigerator magnets and bumper stickers.

19
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:37,000
I'm not opposed to any of those things.

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,920
They're valuable and good, but that is not the sole purpose of Paul's ministry and his teachings.

21
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:46,120
These were real letters to real people.

22
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:52,840
Paul was encouraging them to live faithful lives to the gospel amid difficult circumstances.

23
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:54,120
And we should work hard.

24
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:59,260
This whole series, the whole goal of this series was an interpretive experiment for us,

25
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:03,600
helping us learn how to interpret the Scriptures in their greater context.

26
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:11,380
Our goal should be to understand the original message that Paul intended for the original recipient to hear and receive,

27
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:15,480
and then for us to put that message into practice.

28
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:16,600
Because, guess what?

29
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:24,700
We are also real people with real lives, trying to live faithfully to the gospel of Jesus amid difficult circumstances.

30
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,780
So much of what Paul had to say to first century believers still applies to us.

31
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:33,560
I had a conversation with a guy in my office a couple days ago.

32
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,660
He said, is there a unity problem in our church?

33
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:44,960
He's like, I mean, not any more than any time you have more than three people in a room together.

34
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:46,340
We're pretty good at it.

35
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,920
He's like, well, I feel like we talk about, we've been talking about unity a lot.

36
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:51,400
And he's just asking, like, is there a reason?

37
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,820
Do I feel the need to talk about unity more than other times?

38
00:02:55,820 --> 00:03:00,240
And I said, well, it turns out, and this has been a learning experience for me.

39
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:04,740
It turns out, when you study Paul in his context, when you understand his biography,

40
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:07,800
and then the letters that he's writing, like, so much.

41
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:09,400
A huge majority.

42
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:15,460
I could summarize almost all of what Paul is trying to get at as trying to bring Jews and Gentiles into unity with one another.

43
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:21,340
But we miss that, because we grab paragraphs of letters of Paul's writing,

44
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:25,380
and we dissect these paragraphs apart from their greater context in the book,

45
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:27,340
and apart from the greater context of Paul's ministry.

46
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:31,340
And we grab phrases, and we apply them directly to our lives,

47
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:33,540
apart from what Paul was intending to get at.

48
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:37,080
And so I hope that throughout this series that you've seen,

49
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,380
that reading the Scriptures can be difficult and challenging,

50
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:42,100
but it's worth it.

51
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:48,380
It's so worth it to truly grasp the meaning of exactly what he was trying to say.

52
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,380
So we're almost wrapping up this series.

53
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:52,440
I hope that it's been helpful.

54
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,620
We're going to jump in here in just a minute to Paul's view of salvation.

55
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:56,700
But first, let's pray.

56
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:03,340
Father, we are grateful for the opportunity to just stop.

57
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:06,200
Stop the busyness of life.

58
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:16,220
Cast off the concerns, the worries, the fears, the anxiety, the doubt, the anger, the frustration, the confusion.

59
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:19,840
And just quiet our minds and our souls before you today.

60
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:23,980
May your Holy Spirit do what only your Holy Spirit can do in this room.

61
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:25,100
May you convict us of sin.

62
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:29,440
May you remind us of your love, your grace, your forgiveness,

63
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:34,140
your overwhelming abundant kindness.

64
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:40,100
May you help us to see that living for you truly leads to abundant life,

65
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:42,100
and that everything else will fade away.

66
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:44,680
Everything else is building our life on sand.

67
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:53,140
God, do what only you can do in this place today for your glory and for our good.

68
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:54,900
In Jesus' name I pray.

69
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:55,460
Amen.

70
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:00,420
If you want to follow along with me, I'm going to be in Titus chapter 3 at some point.

71
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:03,740
So Titus chapter 3 is where we will be heading.

72
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:05,060
Who's running computer?

73
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:05,860
Dave, you're running computer.

74
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:06,160
All right.

75
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:07,740
I didn't give you a heads up on this.

76
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,400
Wait for my cue to give the images, okay?

77
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,000
I don't want to give anything away.

78
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,340
No spoilers.

79
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:17,040
We're going to start our message today with some crowd participation.

80
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:18,400
Who loves crowd participation?

81
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:20,200
Yes.

82
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:20,780
All right.

83
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:23,860
So in just a minute, I'm going to put up images on the screen,

84
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:27,880
and what I want you to do is yell out the first thing you see when you see the image.

85
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:28,920
Does that make sense?

86
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:32,920
If you're listening on podcast, this is not going to be helpful for you.

87
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:34,180
So I'm sorry.

88
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:34,580
All right.

89
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:34,960
Let's go.

90
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:35,540
First image.

91
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:36,920
Yell out the first thing you see.

92
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:40,240
Everyone?

93
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:41,980
Wow.

94
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:43,820
We are a very monolithic people.

95
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:44,180
All right.

96
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:48,000
There are faces, and there is a vase in this image.

97
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,400
Sometimes it can be hard to see.

98
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,340
All right.

99
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:52,140
Next image.

100
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:52,800
What do we see?

101
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:55,160
All right.

102
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:56,520
That was a little bit more diverse.

103
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:59,600
We've got a duck, and we've got a bunny, right?

104
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:00,080
A rabbit.

105
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,920
Two different images.

106
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:06,080
All right.

107
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:07,300
Last but not least.

108
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:11,780
This.

109
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:12,380
This.

110
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:12,800
All right.

111
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:14,360
Mother-in-law is in the room.

112
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:18,440
Know that I did not entitle this painting, but the title of this painting is My Wife and

113
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:19,260
My Mother-in-Law.

114
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:27,620
The artist designed this painting so that you can either see a beautiful young woman in a

115
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:32,840
dress or a older woman with a very large nose.

116
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:40,740
This one in particular, once you see one or the other, it's really hard to switch perspectives,

117
00:06:40,740 --> 00:06:41,260
right?

118
00:06:41,260 --> 00:06:46,980
You just kind of get locked in on your image, and you, and Dave, take it down, because they're

119
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:48,400
not going to pay attention to the thing I say.

120
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:52,340
They're trying to show each other the opposite image.

121
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:58,980
These images, they were created by artists to contain multiple images within one.

122
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:04,080
And oftentimes, once we kind of lock in, once our eyes, our mind, our perception locks into

123
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:08,520
one image, it's hard for us to see the other images.

124
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:14,960
And I share, I open with this illustration because I think the American church, we've oversimplified

125
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:15,920
ideas in the Scripture.

126
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:21,340
We've boiled them down to one image, one picture, one thought, one idea.

127
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:27,260
And the Scripture is so much bigger than our one image, one thought, one idea.

128
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:32,140
And often, there's multiple images built into the Scriptures for different topics, different

129
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:38,580
ideas, especially when it comes to the idea of salvation, which is going to be our topic

130
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:39,520
of discussion today.

131
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:44,540
We boil this idea of salvation down to one image, one picture.

132
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:49,760
And I think, I think what Paul has to say about salvation is so much bigger and broader.

133
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:54,260
There's multiple images, multiple facets, lots of things going on in the Scripture when

134
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:56,000
it talks about salvation.

135
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,460
But the American church, we've oversimplified this idea of salvation, and we only, almost

136
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,560
only ever talk about it in the past tense.

137
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:10,240
You go to the church, if you've grown up in church, you often hear people say, I was saved

138
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:17,700
when I was 12, or when I was 15, or at church camp, or after a really bad night in college,

139
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:18,940
or I was saved.

140
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:21,080
When were you saved?

141
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:22,660
Are you guys familiar with this language?

142
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:23,760
Right?

143
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:27,000
We often lock into these images in Scripture.

144
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:33,060
We talk about salvation typically only in the past tense, sometimes in the future tense.

145
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:36,660
And not only that, but we limit this topic of salvation.

146
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:43,900
We limit it to only being the thing that answers our main question, the one question that we

147
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:46,080
want answered when we look to the Bible about salvation.

148
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:50,120
That is, where will I and my loved ones be when we die?

149
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:51,180
Right?

150
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:57,620
This is like the way that we boil down this huge topic in Scripture about salvation.

151
00:08:57,620 --> 00:09:03,140
We boil it down to a past tense experience that answers the question, where will I go when

152
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:03,520
I die?

153
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:06,240
And look, I'm not saying that that Bible doesn't address that.

154
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:08,720
The Bible talks about salvation in the past tense.

155
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:13,640
And the Bible does give us answers to the questions about where we and our loved ones will be when

156
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:14,060
we die.

157
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:18,800
But what I want to make the argument today and over the next three weeks is that's only

158
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:26,520
one image, one small facet of the Bible's grand picture of God's in-progress work of

159
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:32,340
taking rebellious humanity and broken creation and restoring for himself a people who have been

160
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:38,100
redeemed and will inherit for eternity a renewed heaven and a new earth.

161
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:40,000
Corey's excited.

162
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:46,960
My goal for today is to introduce us to Paul's multifaceted and nuanced concepts of salvation.

163
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:50,980
Paul talks about salvation very broadly with broad brushstrokes.

164
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:54,380
And we grab very short phrases that he talks about in salvation.

165
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:58,860
And we put them in theology books and make them answer questions that he wasn't trying to

166
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:01,000
answer in the text that we grabbed them from.

167
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:01,620
Does that make sense?

168
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:02,840
All right.

169
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:05,700
Paul's multifaceted, nuanced concepts of salvation.

170
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:09,140
We're going to dive a bit deeper into one specific facet this morning.

171
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:14,820
And at the end of all of it, I hope our application will be to be in awe and worship of God as we

172
00:10:14,820 --> 00:10:17,700
better understand how great and awesome his salvation is.

173
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:22,280
And I don't have very much time, so we're going to go hard, quick, fast.

174
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:22,580
All right.

175
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:23,560
Titus chapter three.

176
00:10:23,560 --> 00:10:29,800
Starting in verse three, Paul is writing to Titus who he has left to put in leadership over

177
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:30,660
the Christian church.

178
00:10:30,660 --> 00:10:35,960
Once, we too were foolish and disobedient.

179
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:40,040
We were misled and we became slaves to many lusts and pleasures.

180
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:42,740
Our lives were full of evil and envy.

181
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:43,960
We hated each other.

182
00:10:45,340 --> 00:10:55,380
But, when God, our Savior, revealed his kindness and love, he saved us, not because of the righteous

183
00:10:55,380 --> 00:10:58,460
things that we have done, but because of his great mercy.

184
00:10:58,460 --> 00:11:05,880
He washed away our sins, giving us a new birth and a new life through the Holy Spirit.

185
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:10,660
He generously poured out the Spirit upon us through Jesus Christ, our Savior.

186
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:14,460
Because of his grace, he made us right in his sight.

187
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:20,440
He gave us confidence that we will inherit eternal life.

188
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,100
This is the word of the Lord.

189
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:25,140
There we go.

190
00:11:25,140 --> 00:11:25,600
We're getting there.

191
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:26,800
Thanks be to God.

192
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:28,400
Look, it's really hard.

193
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:32,940
It was, I worked really hard for the last two weeks to try and find a passage of Scripture

194
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:38,660
that gives us the general concept of Paul's broad stroke, broad picture of salvation.

195
00:11:38,660 --> 00:11:41,320
And it's really hard to find one passage.

196
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:47,840
And this one doesn't even do it perfectly, but I think it can help us get on the right track.

197
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:52,080
See, Paul regularly talks about salvation in three tenses.

198
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:55,260
He talks about salvation in the past tense.

199
00:11:55,260 --> 00:11:57,060
We were saved.

200
00:11:57,060 --> 00:11:58,260
You were saved.

201
00:11:58,260 --> 00:12:00,560
He talks about salvation in the present tense.

202
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:02,520
You are being saved.

203
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:05,360
And he talks about salvation in the future tense.

204
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:08,460
You will be saved.

205
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:15,640
We can broaden our sources outside of just Paul and say that the entire Bible is one grand narrative

206
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:17,520
about God's plan of salvation.

207
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:18,540
That is ongoing.

208
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:23,860
It is a process where he is restoring humanity and creation back to himself.

209
00:12:23,860 --> 00:12:31,700
Paul, the Bible, talks about salvation as a past tense experience, a present tense thing

210
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:35,020
that is continuing to happen and a future tense thing that will happen.

211
00:12:35,020 --> 00:12:39,160
And the thing that I learned as I studied more this week is that Paul actually rarely talks

212
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:40,760
about it in the past or the present tense.

213
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:44,540
He often focuses most of his attention on the future tense.

214
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:47,380
We will be saved.

215
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:49,020
There will come a day.

216
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:50,620
Christ returns.

217
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:53,180
Every tear will be wiped from our eye.

218
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:57,100
There will be no more mourning, no more pain, no more heartache, no more sin, no more guilt,

219
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:59,880
no more fear, no more depression, no more anxiety.

220
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:03,240
We will be saved.

221
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:07,740
And although we use the language, look, this is where it gets even more complicated.

222
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:10,500
And this is why so many books have been written on this topic.

223
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:17,640
Although we use the language of past, present, and future tense, it's important to note that this is a limiting factor of our human experience.

224
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:20,080
God is not bound by time.

225
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:25,400
The author of salvation is not bound by our restrictions of time.

226
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:27,200
God himself is not linear.

227
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:32,380
So although we experience salvation in a linear way, he does not.

228
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:34,320
He's outside of time.

229
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:44,620
And so the authors of Scripture, they're grasping for any language, any human experience that can help us understand what God has done eternally outside of time.

230
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:51,560
They're trying to help us understand how it works within our limited knowledge and understanding.

231
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:59,600
And every attempt to summarize God's saving work will fall woefully short of what God is actually doing in Christ for you and me.

232
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:06,580
But you all pay me to try, so we're going to try, all right?

233
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:10,960
So really quickly, I want to run down for the next three weeks we're going to be talking about.

234
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:15,620
This week we'll focus on salvation in the past tense, next week in the present tense, week after in the future tense.

235
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:19,400
But let's just set up the concept for the next couple weeks so we're ready.

236
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:31,360
First, salvation is a past tense event that brings enemies of God from every language, tribe, and tongue into the family of God on the basis of faith in Jesus.

237
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:34,120
Remember, we've talked a lot about the word faith.

238
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:38,500
Faith isn't just some empty phrase that you pray one time in the past.

239
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:41,800
Faith is an act of allegiance to the lordship of Jesus.

240
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:48,080
We are brought into the family of God by saying, I want to submit my life to Jesus as Lord.

241
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:50,280
I'm sick and tired of being sick and tired.

242
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:54,620
I'm sick and tired of living under my own authority, my own rule, my own reign.

243
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:55,560
It has not worked.

244
00:14:55,560 --> 00:14:56,540
It is not working.

245
00:14:56,540 --> 00:14:58,260
I'm going to trust Jesus.

246
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:01,640
This is highly relational.

247
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:03,040
It's not just transactional.

248
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:08,920
It's an invitation to be brought into the family of God on the basis of faith.

249
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:18,320
Second, salvation is a present tense experience where in partnership with the Holy Spirit, Christians are continually being renewed into the image of Christ.

250
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:30,600
Salvation in the present tense says, now that you've been brought into the family of God, progressively you begin to model the values of the family of God outwardly.

251
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:35,760
On day one of being brought into the family of God, you are not going to look a whole lot like Jesus.

252
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:42,600
But there's this invitation to grow into that image over time by the power of the Holy Spirit through faith.

253
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:45,720
Three, salvation is a future tense expectation.

254
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:48,300
See how I did the, I kept it with ease.

255
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:49,080
That was good, right?

256
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:51,180
All right, cool.

257
00:15:51,180 --> 00:15:54,680
Well, my salary review is coming up in a couple of months.

